The AdaptiveCpp pass plugin must declare its LLVMPasses dependency.

The plugin registers itself through the new PassBuilder API: it constructs a
PassBuilder, calls PipelineTuningOptions, registerModuleAnalyses,
registerCGSCCAnalyses, registerFunctionAnalyses, registerLoopAnalyses and
crossRegisterProxies, and names OptimizationLevel::O3. Every one of those
lives in LLVMPasses, and the target declares only `clang` and `LLVMSupport`.

With a shared libLLVM the omission is invisible, because the dylib carries the
whole library set. It becomes real when LLVM is built STATICALLY and the
plugin is linked INTO the tools -- LLVM_ADAPTIVECPP_LINK_INTO_TOOLS, which is
how a compiler that ships as one toolchain is built. process_llvm_pass_plugins
then appends the plugin's libraries to EVERY LLVM tool, and a tool that does
not itself link Passes cannot resolve them.

bugpoint is precisely that tool: llvm/tools/bugpoint/CMakeLists.txt lists
Extensions -- so it receives the plugin -- but not Passes, because it predates
the new pass manager. Windows is where this bites first, since upstream builds
no C++ LLVM dylib there, and it would bite any static LLVM on any platform.

Upstream-shaped fix: the plugin declares what it uses. `LLVM_LINK_COMPONENTS`
is what add_llvm_pass_plugin honours, and appending rather than assigning
leaves any components an enclosing scope already set.

Reported to us by win-64 run 34141282374, where LLVM and AdaptiveCpp compiled
in full (7,163 of 7,201 steps) and only bugpoint failed to link.

diff --git a/src/compiler/CMakeLists.txt b/src/compiler/CMakeLists.txt
index 8dd05c0..8202765 100644
--- a/src/compiler/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/src/compiler/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -335,6 +335,15 @@ else()
     )
   endif()
 
+  # The plugin calls into LLVMPasses -- PassBuilder, PipelineTuningOptions,
+  # registerModuleAnalyses and friends, OptimizationLevel -- so it must say so.
+  # With a shared libLLVM the dependency is satisfied invisibly by the dylib,
+  # but when LLVM is built statically and this plugin is linked INTO the tools
+  # (LLVM_ADAPTIVECPP_LINK_INTO_TOOLS), every tool inherits these objects, and
+  # a tool that does not itself link Passes fails to resolve them. bugpoint is
+  # exactly that tool: its LLVM_LINK_COMPONENTS lists Extensions but not
+  # Passes.
+  set(LLVM_LINK_COMPONENTS ${LLVM_LINK_COMPONENTS} Passes)
   add_llvm_pass_plugin(${COMPILER_LIBRARY_NAME}
     NO_MODULE
     SUBPROJECT AdaptiveCpp
